1. d4 g6 2. e4 Bg7 3. Nc3 c6 4. Bc4 d6 5. Qf3 e6 6. Nge2 Nd7 {Another
possibility is} (6... b5 {1/2-1/2 Rublevsky,S-Piket,J/Montecatini Terme 2000/
CBM 79/[Finkel,A] (43)}) 7. Bf4 {White tried also} (7. O-O {See 0-1 Flores,
D-Bologan,V/Buenos Aires 2000/CBM 80/[Finkel,A] (48)}) 7... Ngf6 (7... Qe7 {
possible as well:} 8. Bb3 e5 9. dxe5 dxe5 10. Be3 Ngf6 11. g4 h6 12. g5 hxg5
13. Bxg5 Nc5 14. h4 Bg4 15. Qe3 Be6 16. O-O-O Bxb3 17. axb3 Ne6 18. Kb1 $36 {
Adams,M-Walker,D,Eastbourne ch-GB 1990}) 8. Rd1 ({White cannot win the pawn:} 
8. Bxd6 {, in view of} Nb6 9. e5 Nxc4 10. exf6 Qxf6 $11) 8... Qe7 9. O-O $146 {
According to my database, this position appeared for the first time. In the
previous game white continued} (9. Bg5 h6 10. Bh4 O-O 11. O-O a5 12. a3 e5 13.
d5 g5 14. Bg3 Nb6 15. Bb3 a4 16. Ba2 c5 (16... cxd5 $5) 17. Qd3 Nh5 18. f3 Nf4
$13 {1-0 Ernst,T-Ehlvest,J/Thessaloniki 1988/TD (61)}) 9... O-O 10. Bb3 a5 {
Black suffers from lack of space, therefore, he tries to occupy some space on
the queenside. On the other hand, black's pawn structure is very flexible,
that gives him good chances for counterattack.} 11. Bg5 h6 12. Bh4 e5 {Now
black is defining his pawn structure and immediately is attacking white's
center.} 13. Rfe1 Re8 14. h3 {covering g4 square.} ({White doesn't achieve
anything after} 14. d5 g5 15. dxc6 bxc6 16. Bg3 Nc5 $15) 14... exd4 15. Nxd4
Nc5 $2 {seems very logical, but this move allows white to use some tactical
resources.} ({Better was} 15... Ne5 16. Qe2 (16. Qe3 Nfg4 $1) 16... Qc7 $11 {
[%cal Yf6d7,Yd7c5]}) 16. Bc4 $2 {missing much more attractive option:} (16.
Ndb5 $1 Rd8 $1 (16... cxb5 17. Nd5 $18) (16... Nxb3 17. Nxd6 g5 (17... Rd8 18.
e5 g5 19. exf6 Qxf6 20. Nde4 Qe7 21. Nxg5 $1 $18) 18. Bg3 g4 (18... Nc5 19.
Nxc8 $16) 19. Nxc8 $16) 17. Nxd6 $1 (17. Bxf6 Bxf6 (17... Qxf6 18. Qxf6 Bxf6
19. Nxd6 $16) 18. Nxd6 Nxb3 19. Nxc8 Raxc8 20. axb3 Rxd1 21. Rxd1 Re8) 17...
Rxd6 (17... Nxb3 18. e5 $18) 18. Rxd6 Qxd6 19. e5 Qd2 20. Rd1 $16) 16... Bd7
17. Nb3 Nxb3 18. e5 $5 {Interesting continuation, while simple} (18. Bxb3 {
doesn't promise white anything special.} g5 $6 (18... a4 19. Nxa4 g5 20. Bg3
Nxe4 (20... d5 21. Nb6 $18) 21. Rxe4 $18) (18... Rad8 $142 $1 $11 {[%cal Gd7e6]
}) 19. Bg3 d5 (19... g4 20. Qd3 d5 21. exd5 (21. Bh4 $5) 21... Qxe1+ 22. Rxe1
Rxe1+ 23. Kh2 gxh3 24. gxh3 $14) 20. exd5 Qxe1+ 21. Rxe1 Rxe1+ 22. Kh2 $14)
18... g5 $5 (18... Nc5 {is also possible.} 19. Bxf6 (19. exf6 Qxe1+ 20. Rxe1
Rxe1+ 21. Kh2 Bf8 $17) 19... Bxf6 20. exf6 Qxe1+ 21. Rxe1 Rxe1+ 22. Kh2 $13)
19. Bg3 $2 {Serious mistake, which allows black to get a material advantage. 
[%tqu "Please enter the strongest move for black.","","",g4,"",10]} (19. Bxb3
$2 gxh4 (19... dxe5 20. Bg3 g4 21. Qd3 $15) 20. exf6 Qxf6 21. Qxf6 Rxe1+ $1 (
21... Bxf6 22. Ne4 $13) 22. Rxe1 Bxf6 $17) ({Stronger was} 19. exf6 $1 Qxe1+ (
19... Qxf6 20. Qxf6 Bxf6 21. cxb3 $1 Rxe1+ (21... gxh4 22. Ne4) 22. Rxe1 gxh4
23. Ne4 Be5 24. Nxd6 $11) 20. Rxe1 Rxe1+ 21. Kh2 Nd2 22. Qh5 $1 Nxc4 23. fxg7
Kxg7 (23... Nd2 24. Qxh6 Nf1+ $11) 24. Bxg5 $13) 19... g4 $1 (19... dxe5 20.
Rxe5) (19... Nc5 20. exf6) 20. Qe3 $8 Nc5 21. exf6 Qxf6 22. Qd2 d5 $19 {Now
black is a pawn up without any compensation for white. The rest is a matter of
an easy technique.} 23. Bf1 gxh3 24. gxh3 Qg5 $1 {The simplest way to win the
game. Black trades queens, thus, gets a winning endgame.} 25. Qxg5 hxg5 26. Bd6
Ne6 ({Strong enough as well:} 26... Bxc3 27. Rxe8+ (27. bxc3 Ne4) 27... Rxe8
28. Bxc5 Bxb2 29. Rb1 Be5 30. Rxb7 Bf5 $19) 27. Ne2 (27. Bd3 {doesn't help
white:} Nd4 28. Kg2 Bf5 $19) 27... Bxb2 $1 28. Rb1 Bf6 29. Rxb7 Nd4 {Black
threats with Nf3+ and white has nothing to do against black's activity.} 30.
Rd1 {Other continuations don't change anything:} (30. Kg2 Bf5 31. Nxd4 Bxh3+
32. Kxh3 Rxe1 $19) (30. Reb1 Nf3+ (30... Nxe2+ 31. Bxe2 Rxe2 (31... Bc8 32. Bh5
) 32. Rxd7) 31. Kg2 Nd2 32. Rd1 Nxf1 33. Rxd7 Rxe2 $19) 30... Nxe2+ 31. Bxe2
Bc8 $1 {wining a bishop.} 0-1

