1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bg5 e6 7. g3 $6 {A
strange combination of moves: the bishop on g5 and the fianchetto just do not
seem to fit together.} Be7 8. h4 {A sad but necessary move in order to prevent
the typical ...Nxe4 tactic.} Nc6 (8... b5 9. Bg2 Bb7 10. a3 Nbd7 {Is a "more
Najdorf" approach, and possibly stronger too. However, during the game I was
quite happy with a Scheveningen setup in which the g5-bishop and the h4-pawn
do not stand on the best squares.}) 9. Bg2 Bd7 10. Nb3 Qc7 11. Qe2 {This gives
Black the chance to become very active on the queenside.} (11. a4 {looks
better, although Black would still be doing quite well after} O-O {and 12...Ne5
}) 11... b5 12. O-O O-O (12... h6 $5 {first is interesting, preventing White's
regrouping plan in the game.}) 13. Rad1 Rac8 14. Nb1 Rfd8 15. c3 h6 16. Bc1 e5
$1 {A good plan. White now has problems controlling d5, so little can be done
to prevent Black from achieving his aim in the Sicilian.} 17. N1d2 Bg4 $1 18.
Bf3 {This leaves the bishop poorly placed, but} (18. f3 {would weaken White's
position beyond repair.}) 18... Be6 19. Rfe1 b4 20. Qxa6 bxc3 21. bxc3 Ra8 22.
Qd3 Rxa2 23. Nc4 $6 (23. c4 Nb4 24. Qc3 Rb8 $17) 23... d5 24. exd5 Nxd5 25.
Bxd5 Bxd5 26. Qb1 $2 (26. Nbd2 Bc5 $17) 26... Bxc4 27. Rxd8+ Bxd8 28. Qxa2 Qb6
29. Qa8 Bxb3 30. Be3 Qc7 $19 31. Rb1 Bc2 32. Bb6 Qd7 33. Rc1 Be4 {The game is
over. The rest is just a matter of reaching the time-control on move forty.}
34. Re1 Bf3 35. Kh2 Qd2 36. Rf1 Qd3 37. Re1 Qd2 38. Rf1 Qd3 39. Re1 Kh7 40.
Bxd8 Nxd8 41. Qa5 Qd2 42. Rf1 Qe2 43. Kg1 Be4 0-1

