1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 Bb4+ 4. Bd2 Qe7 5. g3 b6 6. Bg2 Bb7 7. O-O Bxd2 8.
Qxd2 d6 9. Nc3 O-O (9... Ne4 10. Nxe4 Bxe4 11. Qe3 Bb7 12. d5 (12. Rac1 Nd7 13.
b4 O-O 14. d5 e5 15. Nd4 {reaches a position that is clearly of interest for
studying the main game.} Rfe8 16. Nb3 {The b5 square is not convincing here
since black has omitted ...a5 and can so still expel the knight.} Nf6 17. Qd2
Qd7 18. e4 c5 19. bxc5 bxc5 20. Na5 Rab8 21. Rb1 Ba6 22. Rb3 $14 {though
eventually 0-1 Ilincic,Z- Damljanovic,B / Niksic 1996/EXT 99 (70)}) 12... e5 (
12... c5 {is a drastic attempt to avoid having a sitting duck on c7, but black
is not sufficiently prepared to undertake this:} 13. dxe6 fxe6 14. Ng5 Bxg2 15.
Kxg2 {[%csl Re6,Rh7][%cal Ge4a8,Ge4h7,Ge3e4] and because of the additional
possibility of Qe4, black's h7 pawn is as weak as the one on e6.}) 13. b4 $1 {
Amazingly, this move appears to be a novelty. Most games from this position
have put a priority on setting the f-pawn in motion, also a natural plan.
White's choice in the game is equally straightforward - a breakthrough with
c4-c5 or some kind of concessions from black on the queenside. Examining some
of these earlier games gives some food for thought; How much should black fear
the advance of the f-pawn? How should he react to the advance on the queenside?
} (13. Nd2 Nd7 14. f4 O-O 15. f5 Nf6 16. h3 Rac8 (16... Rfc8 17. g4 h6 18. g5
hxg5 19. Qxg5 Kf8 20. Rf3 c6 21. Rg3 cxd5 $1 $36 {Neverov-Kiselev, Podolsk
GMA-qual 1989, 0-1, 40.}) 17. g4 h6 18. Qd3 c6 19. dxc6 Bxc6 20. Bxc6 Rxc6 21.
e4 Rfc8 $132 {1/2-1/2 Arbakov,V-Kiselev,S/Moscow 1989/EXT 99 (59). These games
indicate that organizing ...c6 is a more concrete achievement than white's
kingside being flung forward.}) 13... Nd7 14. Nd2 $1 a5 (14... O-O 15. Nb3 Nf6
{was possible, and similar to Ilincic,Z - Damljanovic,B Niksic 1996 given
above. It is understandable that black seeks a more active plan, but the
advance of the a-pawn has several drawbacks; it is not clear that the
possibility of opening the a-file is not a relative advantage for white, and
more importantly, the b5 square becomes a tempting outpost for the white
knight.}) 15. a3 O-O (15... a4 $5 {deserves consideration, though the a-pawn
is most likely to be a long-term weakness.} 16. Ne4 O-O 17. Nc3 $14) 16. Nb3 {
[%csl Ga5,Gc5]} a4 ({Black cannot really stand his ground due to the frailty
of his a-pawn.} 16... Rfe8 17. bxa5 (17. c5 bxc5 18. Nxa5 Reb8 19. Nc6 (19.
Rfc1) 19... Bxc6 20. dxc6 Nb6 $132) 17... bxa5 18. a4 Nb6 19. Qc3 Bc8 20. c5 $1
$16 ({The greedy} 20. Nxa5 Bd7 {gives black annoying counterplay against the
a-pawn.})) ({Grovelling with} 16... axb4 17. axb4 Rxa1 18. Rxa1 Ra8 {was
perhaps the most logical course.}) 17. Nd4 $1 Qd8 18. Nb5 {[%csl Ra4,Rc7,Rd6]
Very strong. This piece cripples black's entire queenside.} Ba6 $6 {Although
the pressure against c7 is extremely annoying, taking on b5 doesn't help and
black should be searching for an alternative plan. Perhaps} (18... Nf6 {
planning ...Qd7.}) 19. Qc3 f5 20. Qc2 Bxb5 (20... Bb7 21. Nc3 (21. Bh3 $5) (21.
f4)) 21. cxb5 $16 {[%csl Rc7,Rf5][%cal Gh3e6,Ge6g8] Black's position is now
too unpleasant to handle. The a- and c-pawns are frail, and white can easily
mount additional pressure against the f-pawn, incidentally threatening to open
more lines and tear into the position with the B.} Rf7 {Hoping to erect a
defensive shell with ...Nf8, but white can prevent this.} 22. Bh3 $1 e4 (22...
g6 23. e4) 23. f3 $1 {Simple and effective - black has no reply to this
increase of force on his position. White owns the entire board.} Nf8 24. fxe4
Qe8 25. Rxf5 Rxf5 26. Bxf5 Qxb5 27. Rf1 (27. Qxc7 Qxe2 28. Rf1 {looks like the
most direct method, though it does allow counterplay;} Qe3+ 29. Kg2 g6 30. Be6+
Nxe6 31. Qf7+ Kh8 32. Qf6+ $5 Kg8 33. dxe6 {and the e-pawn should score the
point.}) 27... Ra7 28. e5 {White finds another way to increase the energy
level of his position.} (28. Qd3 {was the tedious way to proceed.}) 28... Qxd5
(28... dxe5 29. d6 c5 (29... cxd6 30. Bxh7+ Nxh7 31. Qc8+ Nf8 32. Rxf8+ Kh7 33.
Rh8+ Kg6 34. Qe6+) 30. Bd3 $18) 29. Be4 Qd4+ 30. e3 $1 {[%cal Ga2g8] Regaining
control of c4/d5 and thereby renewing threats of deadly checks on the diagonal.
} Qxe3+ 31. Kg2 Nd7 32. Bd5+ (32. Qc4+ Kh8 33. e6 Nf6 34. e7 {is crushing and})
(32. Bxh7+ Kh8 33. Rf7 {is not bad either.}) 32... Kh8 33. Qf5 $4 {1-0 (33)
Tukmakov,V (2605)-Onischuk,A (2630)/Donetsk 1998/CBM 068/[Tisdall] How very
strange - this is black's best position of virtually the entire game:} (33. Qf5
Qd2+ {and ...Qxd5 defends the mate on the last rank and leaves black something
to live for. One has to doubt the accuracy of the gamescore or picture a
raging time scramble.} 34. Kg1 Qxd5 35. Qxd7 Ra8 36. e6 {and black should be
able to arrange perpetual check.}) (33. Bc6 {wins without problem.})) 10. d5 e5
11. e4 a5 12. Ne1 (12. Nh4 {para jugar Tae1 y f4 es un plan factible}) 12...
Na6 13. Nc2 (13. Nd3 Nd7 14. f4 Nac5 15. Nxc5 Nxc5 16. Rae1 a4) 13... Nc5 14.
Rab1 Rfe8 15. b3 Ba6 16. Qd1 (16. a3 a4 (16... Qd7 17. Ne3 a4 18. b4 Nb3 19.
Qd3 Nd4 20. f4) 17. b4 Nb3 18. Qd3) 16... Reb8 17. a3 Nxb3 $6 ({Es mejor} 17...
Bc8 18. Ne3 Bd7) 18. Rxb3 Bxc4 19. Rb1 Bxf1 20. Bxf1 Nd7 21. Bb5 Rc8 (21... Rf8
) 22. Bc6 (22. Bxd7 $1 Qxd7 23. Nb5) 22... Rab8 23. Qe2 (23. Bxd7 Qxd7 24. Nb5)
23... Rf8 (23... Nc5 24. h4 g6 25. Ne3 Rf8) 24. Bxd7 Qxd7 25. Qb5 Qh3 (25...
Qxb5 26. Nxb5 f6 27. Ne3 Rf7 28. Rc1 g6 29. Rc6 Rb7 30. g4 Kh8 31. Kg2 Kg7 32.
Kg3 Rd7 33. h4 Re7 34. f4 Rf7 35. f5) 26. Ne3 g6 27. Rc1 f5 $2 (27... f6 {Para
aguantar la posicion con Tf7 es mas recomendable}) 28. Qd7 $18 Rf7 (28... Kh8
29. exf5 gxf5 30. Nb5 $18) 29. Qe6 Kf8 30. Nb5 $1 Re8 31. Nxc7 $1 Rxe6 (31...
Rxc7 32. Qf6+ Rf7 33. Qh8+ Ke7 34. Rc7+ Kd8 35. Rc8+ Kxc8 36. Qxe8+ $18) 32.
Nxe6+ Ke8 33. Rc8+ Ke7 34. Rc7+ Ke8 35. Rxf7 f4 36. Nc4 1-0

